{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1087", "sample_idx": 20, "model_name": "mistralai/mistral-small-4-119b-2603", "content": "the social network (2010) is a masterclass in modern storytelling, blending sharp dialogue, intricate plotting, and a scathing dissection of ambition, betrayal, and the cost of success. directed by david fincher with a script by aaron sorkin, the film chronicles the rise of facebook and its enigmatic founder, mark zuckerberg, played with icy precision by jesse eisenberg. the movie isn’t just about a social media empire—it’s about the fractures in human relationships, the way technology reshapes (or corrodes) connections, and the ruthless nature of innovation.\n\nthe script is a marvel, packed with rapid-fire exchanges that crackle with wit and venom. sorkin’s dialogue feels like a duel, where every line carries weight, whether it’s zuckerberg’s condescending barbs or the legal team’s calculated threats. the pacing is relentless, mirroring the breakneck speed of the digital revolution while never losing sight of the personal toll. the film’s structure—jumping between lawsuits, flashbacks, and zuckerberg’s isolated genius—creates a sense of inevitability, as if the empire’s creation was always doomed to leave casualties in its wake.\n\njesse eisenberg’s performance is a revelation. he captures zuckerberg’s brilliance and social ineptitude with unsettling accuracy, making the character both sympathetic and infuriating. andrew garfield’s eduardo savarin is the emotional core, his quiet devastation at zuckerberg’s betrayal serving as a counterpoint to the cold, cerebral world of facebook. armie hammer’s twins, cameron and tyler winklevoss, add a layer of dark comedy, their privileged arrogance clashing hilariously with zuckerberg’s passive-aggressive genius. even minor roles, like max minghella’s divya narendra or brendan gleeson’s relentless lawyer, are unforgettable.\n\nfincher’s direction is sleek and controlled, with a color palette that’s cool and clinical, reflecting the sterile, transactional nature of silicon valley. the editing—particularly kirk baxter and angus wall’s work—is seamless, cutting between past and present with a precision that mirrors how digital connections collapse time and memory. trent reznor and atticus ross’s score is a haunting, electronic pulse that underscores the film’s themes of isolation and surveillance.\n\nthe social network isn’t just a bio-pic; it’s a tragedy disguised as a success story. it doesn’t glorify innovation but instead asks: at what point does ambition become exploitation? the film’s final scene—zuckerberg checking out a girl on facebook, only to be met with the same digital void he created—is a devastating punchline. it’s a story about a man who changed the world but couldn’t find a place in it, and that’s what makes it so compelling.\n\nif you love dialogue-driven dramas with razor-sharp writing and morally ambiguous characters, this is a must-watch. if you’re looking for a feel-good underdog tale, well… maybe try something else. but if you want a film that dissects the dark heart of the digital age with style and substance, the social network delivers in spades. 9.5/10.", "logprobs": [{"token": "the", "logprob": -0.37151509523391724}, {"token": " social", "logprob": -0.004369948524981737}, {"token": " network", "logprob": -2.3841855067985307e-07}, {"token": " (", "logprob": -1.296724796295166}, {"token": "2", "logprob": -0.00113589596003294}, {"token": "0", "logprob": -2.145764938177308e-06}, {"token": "1", "logprob": -2.145764938177308e-06}, {"token": "0", "logprob": -6.6756979322235566e-06}, {"token": ")", "logprob": -0.6327029466629028}, {"token": " is", "logprob": -0.2367950677871704}, {"token": " a", "logprob": -0.016613824293017387}, {"token": " master", "logprob": -0.599931538105011}, {"token": "class", "logprob": -0.9979985952377319}, {"token": " in", "logprob": -3.6238969187252223e-05}, {"token": " modern", "logprob": -0.643555760383606}, {"token"